> All the patch does is make the "player is 
>changing levels so write the recoverable information to disk" routine 
>run when the player restores a save file. Ordinarily, if nethack crashes 
>after the player loads a save file and before the player changes levels 
>the player loses his game. This patch prevents that. 

It seems that if they had wanted it to work that way, they would have
done it that way.  

Oh, here's the catch.  It's not a problem on a single-user system; it is
on multi-user systems.

If it is the user's own kill, the savefile should not be available.
Allowing the emergency save in this instance lets a player, under his
own control, rewind the clock by using the kill command.  There are
other ways to accomplish this on a system you control, but not as a
mere user on the multi-user system.

I believe that if you change levels and end the game with a kill, you
still don't get the saved game--but it's been a very long time since I
tried (seems to me it was after something landed on the keyboard).

